# FSE Templates, Parts & Patterns

| Directory | Contains | Format |
|---|---|---|
| `templates/` | Page-level layouts (`single.html`, `archive-{type}.html`) | HTML block markup |
| `parts/` | Reusable areas referenced by `wp:template-part` | HTML block markup |
| `patterns/` | Insertable content layouts, auto-registered | PHP with a header comment |
| `partials/` | PHP fragments rendered by classes and hooks | PHP |

`patterns/` and `partials/` are different things. Patterns are editor-insertable
block markup; partials are PHP output called from a class or hook. Don't put
block markup in `partials/` or PHP logic in `patterns/`.

**Never lint or format `templates/`, `parts/`, or `patterns/`.** Gutenberg owns
that markup. `npm run format`, Prettier, ESLint, stylelint, and PHPCS must not
rewrite it. This is the theme-root directories only — `src/sass/patterns/` is
hand-written Sass and stays in stylelint.

## Template shape

Templates stay thin — a header part, a `main` group, a footer part — and compose
their content from patterns rather than inlining markup:

```html
<!-- wp:template-part {"slug":"header","theme":"skaflow","tagName":"header"} /-->

<!-- wp:group {"tagName":"main","layout":{"type":"constrained"}} -->
<main class="wp-block-group">
	<!-- wp:pattern {"slug":"skaflow/page-title"} /-->
</main>
<!-- /wp:group -->

<!-- wp:template-part {"slug":"footer","theme":"skaflow","tagName":"footer"} /-->
```

The `theme` attribute in `wp:template-part` carries the theme slug and is part of
the rename surface.

## Patterns

Auto-registered from `patterns/` — no PHP registration needed. Every file needs a
header comment:

```php
<?php
/**
 * Title: Human-readable name
 * Slug: skaflow/slug-here
 * Description: One sentence describing the pattern and its style.
 * Categories: section, skaflow
 * Keywords: keyword1, keyword2
 */
?>
```

`Title`, `Slug`, `Description` and `Categories` are all required. Missing
`Description` and `Keywords` degrade discoverability in the inserter.

**Only declare categories that are registered.** The theme registers its own in
`lib/class-after-setup-theme.php` via `register_block_pattern_category()`;
WordPress core provides the rest (`banner`, `about`, `call-to-action`, `footer`,
`header`, `posts`, `query`, `text`, `gallery`, `columns`, `featured`,
`testimonials`, `services`, `contact`, `team`, `portfolio`, `media`). Declaring
an unregistered category silently drops the pattern out of that filter. Which
categories this project registers — and which are retired — is in `PROJECT.md`.

## Images — theme assets only

Never leave a `/wp-content/uploads/` URL or a `wp_get_attachment_url()` call in a
pattern. Uploads are site-specific; a pattern must work on any install of the
theme.

```php
<img src="<?php echo esc_url( get_theme_file_uri( 'assets/images/placeholder.png' ) ); ?>" alt="placeholder"/>
```

Also strip `wp-image-{ID}` classes, `class="size-full"`, and real `mediaId`
values that the editor leaves behind. If a needed image isn't in `assets/images/`,
add it there rather than referencing an upload.

## Shortcodes in block markup

The theme registers a shortcode for reading ACF fields inside otherwise static
block markup, useful in patterns bound to a configured field:

```html
<!-- wp:heading -->
<h2 class="wp-block-heading">[skaflow field="hero_title"]</h2>
<!-- /wp:heading -->
```

Registered in `lib/class-shortcodes.php`. Prefer real block bindings or a
dynamic block where the content is structural; the shortcode is for content
authored once in options.

